Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream stream(markdown);

    while (std::getline(stream, line)) {
        html += line + "\n";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Title\n\nThis is a markdown text.\n\nAnother line.\n";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends each line to the `html` string. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function by converting a sample markdown text to HTML and printing the result.

Note that this implementation does not support all markdown features, such as images and links. It only supports basic markdown features like headings and paragraphs.

